Write "w /dev/stdout" through stdout itself.
The w command and the s///w flag opened /dev/stdout as a new stream,
with a buffer of its own, so what they wrote could come out before or
after the normal output of the same cycle instead of in order. On Linux,
when stdout was redirected to a file, opening /dev/stdout for writing
also truncated that file, and the two streams then wrote at separate
offsets, overwriting each other. GNU sed treats the name specially. Do
the same: use stdout for /dev/stdout, without opening anything.

---
 editors/sed.c |    4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -245,6 +245,10 @@
 {
 	struct sed_FILE **pp = &G.FILE_head;
 	struct sed_FILE *cur;
+
+	/* Share stdout's buffer, so that the output stays in order */
+	if (strcmp(fname, "/dev/stdout") == 0)
+		return stdout;
 	while ((cur = *pp) != NULL) {
 		if (strcmp(cur->fname, fname) == 0)
 			return cur->fp;
